## Coequalizer of Two Maps in a Category

### Definition

Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category and let $\alpha, \alpha' : M \rightrightarrows N$ be two parallel morphisms. A **coequalizer** of $\alpha$ and $\alpha'$ is a morphism

$$q : N \to Q$$

such that:

1. **(Cocone condition)** $q \circ \alpha = q \circ \alpha'$, i.e., the following diagram commutes:

$$M \underset{\alpha'}{\stackrel{\alpha}{\rightrightarrows}} N \stackrel{q}{\longrightarrow} Q$$

2. **(Universality)** For any object $X$ and any morphism $f : N \to X$ with $f \circ \alpha = f \circ \alpha'$, there exists a **unique** morphism $\bar{f} : Q \to X$ making the following diagram commute:

$$M \underset{\alpha'}{\stackrel{\alpha}{\rightrightarrows}} N \stackrel{q}{\longrightarrow} Q \stackrel{\bar f}{\longrightarrow} X$$

i.e., $f = \bar{f} \circ q$.

Equivalently, $q : N \to Q$ is the **colimit** of the diagram consisting of the two parallel arrows $\alpha, \alpha' : M \rightrightarrows N$. It is the "most efficient" way to identify (force equal) the two images $\alpha(m)$ and $\alpha'(m)$ for all $m \in M$.

Coequalizers, when they exist, are unique up to unique isomorphism.

---

### Example 1: In the category $R\text{-}\mathbf{Mod}$ of (left) $R$-modules

Let $\alpha, \alpha' : M \rightrightarrows N$ be two $R$-module homomorphisms. Define

$$\delta := \alpha - \alpha' : M \to N, \qquad \delta(m) = \alpha(m) - \alpha'(m).$$

This is an $R$-module homomorphism. Let

$$Q := N / \operatorname{im}(\delta) = N / \operatorname{im}(\alpha - \alpha'),$$

and let

$$q : N \to Q, \qquad q(n) = n + \operatorname{im}(\alpha - \alpha')$$

be the canonical quotient (projection) homomorphism.

**Verification of the cocone condition.** For every $m \in M$:

$$q(\alpha(m)) = \alpha(m) + \operatorname{im}(\delta), \qquad q(\alpha'(m)) = \alpha'(m) + \operatorname{im}(\delta).$$

Since $\alpha(m) - \alpha'(m) = \delta(m) \in \operatorname{im}(\delta)$, the two cosets coincide:

$$q \circ \alpha = q \circ \alpha'.$$

**Verification of universality.** Let $f : N \to X$ be any $R$-module homomorphism with $f \circ \alpha = f \circ \alpha'$. Then for all $m \in M$:

$$f(\alpha(m)) = f(\alpha'(m)) \implies f(\alpha(m) - \alpha'(m)) = 0 \implies f(\delta(m)) = 0.$$

Hence $\operatorname{im}(\delta) \subseteq \ker(f)$. By the **universal property of the quotient module**, there exists a unique $R$-module homomorphism $\bar{f} : N/\operatorname{im}(\delta) \to X$ with $f = \bar{f} \circ q$, namely:

$$\bar{f}(n + \operatorname{im}(\delta)) = f(n).$$

This is well-defined precisely because $\operatorname{im}(\delta) \subseteq \ker(f)$, and unique because $q$ is surjective. $\checkmark$

**Summary for $R$-modules:** The coequalizer of $\alpha, \alpha' : M \rightrightarrows N$ is the quotient

$$\boxed{N \twoheadrightarrow N/\operatorname{im}(\alpha - \alpha')}.$$

---

### Example 2: In the category $\mathbf{Set}$ of sets

Let $\alpha, \alpha' : M \rightrightarrows N$ be two functions between sets. Define the **equivalence relation $\sim$ on $N$** generated by declaring

$$\alpha(m) \sim \alpha'(m) \quad \text{for all } m \in M.$$

Concretely, $\sim$ is the **smallest equivalence relation** on $N$ containing the relation

$$R_0 = \big\{(\alpha(m),\, \alpha'(m)) : m \in M\big\} \subseteq N \times N.$$

That is, $\sim$ is the reflexive–symmetric–transitive closure of $R_0$. Two elements $n_1, n_2 \in N$ satisfy $n_1 \sim n_2$ if and only if there is a finite zig-zag

$$n_1 = \alpha(m_1) \sim \alpha'(m_1) = \alpha(m_2) \sim \alpha'(m_2) = \cdots = n_2$$

(or $n_1 = n_2$, or the reverse direction, etc.).

Set

$$Q := N/{\sim}, \qquad q : N \to Q, \quad q(n) = [n]_{\sim}$$

the canonical projection sending each element to its equivalence class.

**Verification of the cocone condition.** For every $m \in M$, by construction $\alpha(m) \sim \alpha'(m)$, so

$$q(\alpha(m)) = [\alpha(m)] = [\alpha'(m)] = q(\alpha'(m)).$$

Thus $q \circ \alpha = q \circ \alpha'$. $\checkmark$

**Verification of universality.** Let $f : N \to X$ be any function with $f \circ \alpha = f \circ \alpha'$, i.e., $f(\alpha(m)) = f(\alpha'(m))$ for all $m \in M$. Then $f$ is constant on each generating pair $(\alpha(m), \alpha'(m))$. Since $f$ respects the generating relation $R_0$, and $\sim$ is the smallest equivalence relation containing $R_0$, it follows that $f$ is constant on each entire $\sim$-equivalence class:

$$n_1 \sim n_2 \implies f(n_1) = f(n_2).$$

(Proof: $f$ being constant on $R_0$ means $f$ descends through the equivalence relation **generated** by $R_0$; equivalently, the kernel equivalence relation $\ker f = \{(n_1, n_2) : f(n_1) = f(n_2)\}$ is an equivalence relation containing $R_0$, hence contains $\sim$.)

Therefore we may define

$$\bar{f} : Q = N/{\sim} \to X, \qquad \bar{f}([n]) = f(n).$$

This is well-defined because $f$ is constant on each equivalence class, and it is unique because $q$ is surjective (so $\bar{f}$ is determined by $\bar{f} \circ q = f$). Moreover $f = \bar{f} \circ q$ by construction. $\checkmark$

**Summary for $\mathbf{Set}$:** The coequalizer of $\alpha, \alpha' : M \rightrightarrows N$ is the quotient set

$$\boxed{N \twoheadrightarrow N/{\sim}}$$

where $\sim$ is the equivalence relation generated by $\alpha(m) \sim \alpha'(m)$ for all $m \in M$.

---

### Remark on the common pattern

In both examples, the coequalizer is obtained as a **quotient of $N$**:

- In $R\text{-}\mathbf{Mod}$, we quotient by the submodule $\operatorname{im}(\alpha - \alpha')$. The algebraic structure lets us linearize the difference $\alpha - \alpha'$, and quotients of modules automatically close under the module operations, so no "transitive closure" step is needed—the image of a single homomorphism is already a submodule.

- In $\mathbf{Set}$, there is no algebraic operation to combine $\alpha(m)$ and $\alpha'(m)$, so we must take the **equivalence relation generated** by the pairs $(\alpha(m), \alpha'(m))$, which includes the reflexive, symmetric, and transitive closure.

In both cases, the coequalizer universally forces $\alpha$ and $\alpha'$ to become equal, and any other map $f$ that already identifies them factors uniquely through the quotient.

### PROOF COMPLETE
